Replaces the qualitative 'honest grades — not everything is A+'
guidance in §3.8 with an explicit per-axis rubric and a numeric
formula for C_Σ.

Per-axis rubric maps grades A through <C to numeric values 4.0
through <2.0, keyed off:
  - whether all ACs were met
  - count of binding findings (zero / ≤1 / ≥2)
  - presence of wiring or honest-claim class findings
  - rounds of REQUEST CHANGES
  - protocol-completeness (ledger row, PRA, provenance attachment)

C_Σ = geometric mean of the three numeric grades; report closest
letter.

Motivation: the supercycle's retroactive grading of v0.4.0 produced
α B / β C+ / γ C / C_Σ C+. The grades were defensible but the
existing §3.8 left them unanchored — any reviewer could pick
different letters without a shared rubric. Making the rubric
explicit gives the next post-release assessment a reproducible
target and gives gamma a defense when arguing against grade
inflation.

Anti-patterns expanded: 'score the intent rather than what shipped'
and 'round up because the team worked hard' are explicit fails.
